# dependencies
node_modules

# build output
dist
dist-ssr
*.local

# Local env (Supabase URL + publishable key). The publishable key is public-safe, but keep env
# out of git; production values live in the Cloudflare Pages dashboard. .env.example is committed.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# editor / os
.DS_Store
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json

# logs
*.log
npm-debug.log*

# Tauri
src-tauri/target
src-tauri/gen

# Foundry source clone for the data importer (not tracked)
.import-src/

# Generated ast description trees: ship ONLY the gzipped buckets (`public/ast/*.json.gz`) — the raw JSON
# is ~88 MB and individual files exceed static-host per-file limits. The runtime loader fetches the .gz
# and decompresses. `idmap.json` is a build-time-only artifact (the importer bakes refs into the ast).
public/ast/*.json
public/idmap.json
# The importer's pristine Foundry transcription reference (~18 MB) — a LOCAL build reference, never shipped.
public/core.foundry-backup.json
# Initiative-tracker bestiary/stat-block data (~51 MB) is now TRACKED — do not re-ignore it. It has
# to be in the repo for the WEB tracker to have a bestiary at all: Cloudflare Pages builds only from
# the repo, so while it was ignored "Add Combatants" came up empty on the website. The installers were
# never affected (a local `vite build` copies public/data → dist/data, which Tauri embeds), which is
# why the gap only ever showed on the web. Size is fine: the largest single file is 4.2 MB (GitHub
# warns at 50 MB, Cloudflare caps at 25 MB/file), and tracker/src/data/dataStore.ts fetches per-book
# on demand, so a visitor downloads index.json (~1.6 MB) plus only the books they actually open.

# Local audit/migration tooling & working-data (~19 MB reassessment/patch/chunk JSONs) — not needed for
# build or runtime (the compiled overlay ships as scripts/data/effect-backfill.json). Keep only the two
# render-quality SCANNERS, which are the guardrails for the description pipeline.
scripts/audit/*
!scripts/audit/ast-coverage.mjs
!scripts/audit/ast-artifacts.mjs
# Feat-audit INPUTS and RESULTS are durable and must survive. The frozen sample is only meaningful if
# it never changes, and the extraction/verification outputs cost real tokens to reproduce. The large
# derived files (feat-500-evidence.json, builder-500-evidence.json, audit-500-evidence.json,
# gold-text-only.json) stay ignored — every one of them regenerates in minutes.
!scripts/audit/feat-500.json
# The LEVEL-ORDERED work lists and their results (2026-08-13 onward). `batch-NNN.json` is the input —
# 100 feats in (level, id) order — and `batch-NNN-result.json` is what the run judged. Both are
# durable: a batch is only meaningful if it never changes, and a verdict costs real tokens. The
# derived evidence beside them (batch-NNN-evidence.json and its two halves) stays ignored — it
# regenerates in minutes from the two harnesses.
!scripts/audit/batch-[0-9][0-9][0-9].json
!scripts/audit/batch-[0-9][0-9][0-9]-result.json
# The audit's own definition — the prompts ARE the instrument, and a run is only reproducible if the
# prompt that produced it is in the history. Same reason bakeoff.js is kept.
!scripts/audit/audit-500.js
# audit-batch.js is audit-500.js with the work list handed in — derived, never hand-copied, so the
# level-ordered run asks byte-identical questions.
!scripts/audit/audit-batch.js
!scripts/audit/feat-text-defects.json
!scripts/audit/lane-gaps.json
!scripts/audit/gold-candidates.json
!scripts/audit/gold-freetext.json
!scripts/audit/residual-findings.json
!scripts/audit/bakeoff.js
!scripts/audit/system-candidates.json
!scripts/audit/gold-extraction.json
!scripts/audit/gold-answer-key.json
!scripts/audit/gold-adjudication.json
!scripts/audit/gold-final.json
!scripts/audit/gold-set.json
!scripts/audit/audit-500-result.json
!scripts/audit/tier-result.json
!scripts/audit/uncertain-settled.json
!scripts/audit/authored-verification.json
!scripts/audit/authored-fix-recheck.json
# The diagnosis of that recheck's 6 open findings (2026-08-13, 12 agents / 1.9M tokens): current state
# of each, the remedy, and the refutation that broke 5 of the 6. Durable because the refutations found
# live defects nobody had a finding for, and because re-deriving it costs another 1.9M.
!scripts/audit/recheck-diagnosis.json
scripts/aon-verify/
tmptest/
# Probe/measurement one-offs written while diagnosing. They are how a finding gets MEASURED rather than
# asserted, and they are worth writing — but they are working notes, not deliverables. Anything worth
# keeping graduates into scripts/ with a header explaining what it measures and why.
scratchpad/

# Android release signing — NEVER commit the keystore or its passwords.
# BACK THESE UP somewhere safe: losing the keystore means release updates can no longer be installed.
signing/
tsconfig.tsbuildinfo

# Wanderer's Guide database dump — GPL-3.0 material, kept LOCAL as a comparison source only.
# Never commit it: this repo ships proprietary, and vendoring GPL data here would relicense the app.
work/wg/

# Pre-change snapshots. These are local safety copies, not history: the data they
# guard is verified by the guards in `npm run verify` (bestiary-fields-check,
# render-check, aonid-integrity, classfeature-page-check, edition-drift-check,
# process-parity). Committing them would add ~125 MB of duplicate JSON.
work/*.bak
public/*.bak
public/data.pre-bestiary.bak/
out.txt

# Regenerable batch intermediates from the Wanderer's Guide parity pass. The
# scripts that produce them ARE tracked; their outputs are not.
work/wg-batch-*.json
# The experience gate's raw harness dumps and the sweep aggregate — regenerated by scripts/wg-experience.mjs.
work/.experience-*.json
